      "content": "# Computer Basics: A Practical Introduction to Systems and Software - source capture\n\nNotebook: https://notebook.google.com/notebook/15f8ab87-e0dc-40a2-899c-dbdce3b8a106\nCaptured: 2026-09-30T20:54:18.086Z | walker: notebooklm-ingestion extension | sources: 7/7 | text chars: 297366 | page images: 39 | summaries: 7\nProvenance: research/captures/notebooklm_sources/02_Computer_Basics_A_Practical_Introduction_to_Systems_and_Software_15f8ab87.json sha256 11382CAFCE0E731F859B53067DCA05BE29FF44C8A98D143DD40565CCD54E4681\n\n## 1. Assembly Basics: The Language Behind the Hardware\n\nType: doc | text chars: 12642 | page images: 0 | summary chars: 858\n\n### Summary\n\nThis educational transcript provides a comprehensive introduction to **Assembly language**, characterizing it as the essential **low-level bridge** between human-readable code and a computer's physical hardware. The text meticulously breaks down the architectural components of the language, explaining how **mnemonics and operands** interact with **CPU registers and status flags** to manage data with extreme precision. By detailing the **fetch-decode-execute cycle**, it illustrates the mechanical process of how a processor translates these specific instructions into functional tasks. While the source highlights the **unrivaled performance and control** necessary for systems like device drivers and embedded electronics, it also balances this by noting the **limitations of complexity and lack of portability** across different hardware architectures.\n\n### Text\n\nEver wondered how your computer\nactually understands the code you write?\nOr how programs run at the lowest level,\ninteracting directly with the hardware?\nYou’re in the right place.\nWelcome to WhiteboardDoodles,\nthe channel where we simplify\ntech-related concepts\nusing whiteboard animations.\nToday, we’ll explore Assembly language,\nthe essential building block for how computers\nexecute instructions at the hardware level.\nLet's get started.\nFirst, let's take a look at what Assembly is.\nAssembly language is a low-level\nprogramming language that acts as a crucial bridge\nbetween the code we write and the machine code\nthat a computer’s hardware understands.\nUnlike high-level languages like Python or Java,\nwhich are designed to be easier for humans\nto read and write, Assembly language\nis much closer to the computer's hardware.\nThis gives programmers direct control\nover the CPU and memory, allowing them\nto optimize how the machine performs tasks.\nEach instruction in assembly tells the computer\nto perform a very specific operation,\nlike moving data between registers,\nperforming arithmetic or jumping to a new instruction.\nBecause of its precision, Assembly is used in areas\nwhere performance and control are critical,\nsuch as in embedded systems, game consoles,\ndevice drivers and even operating systems.\nThough it can be more challenging to learn,\nunderstanding Assembly gives you a deeper insight\ninto how computers really work at their core.\nNow, let's take a look\nat the basic components of Assembly.\nThe basic components of Assembly language\nare the building blocks that allow you\nto communicate directly\nwith the computer's hardware.\nThese include instructions, which tell the computer\nwhat action to perform, and operands,\nwhich specify the data the instruction will work on.\nAssembly uses mnemonics, that is, human-readable\nabbreviations like MOV, ADD or JMP\nto represent these instructions.\nEach instruction operates on registers,\nwhich are small fast storage areas inside the CPU\nwhere data is temporarily held during operations.\nIt's important to note that assembly syntax\ndepends on the architecture of the device\nyou’re programming, which means\nthat code written for one type of CPU,\nlike x86, won't work for another, such as ARM.\nTogether, these components give programmers\nfine-tuned control over how the computer\nhandles tasks, allowing for efficient\nand precise execution.\nNext let's take a closer look at the CPU registers.\nCPU registers are small, high-speed storage locations\nwithin the processor that hold data and instructions\ntemporarily during computation.\nThey play a crucial role in the execution of programs,\nas they provide the fastest means\nfor the CPU to access and manipulate data.\nThere are different kinds of registers,\neach designed for specific purposes,\nsuch as general-purpose registers\n(which can hold data for various operations),\nspecial-purpose registers\n(like the instruction register, which holds\nthe current instruction being executed\nand the program counter, which keeps track\nof the address of the next instruction)\nand status registers\n(which maintain information about the current state\nof the CPU, including flags for arithmetic operations).\nFor example, consider a simple operation\nwhere you want to add two numbers.\nThe CPU might first load the first number\ninto a general-purpose register\n(let's say Register A)\nand the second number into another\ngeneral-purpose register - Register B.\nThe addition operation is then performed\nusing these registers, with the result stored\nin a third register - Register C.\nThis use of registers allows the CPU to perform\ncalculations rapidly, significantly enhancing\noverall performance.\nNow, let's briefly discuss flags in Assembly.\nFlags are special bits in the CPU\nthat store the results of operations\nand help control the flow of a program.\nThese status flags are updated automatically\nbased on the outcome of instructions\nand are used to make decisions during execution.\nFor example, the Zero Flag is set\nwhen the result of an operation is zero,\nwhile the Carry Flag is set if an arithmetic operation\nproduces a result too large for the register to hold.\nThe Overflow Flag tracks whether\nan arithmetic operation results in an overflow\nand the Sign Flag indicates\nwhether the result is negative.\nFlags are especially important\nin conditional operations; for instance,\nafter a comparison, a jump instruction will check\nthe status of these flags to determine whether or not\nto jump to another part of the code.\nUnderstanding flags is key to controlling\nhow the CPU processes tasks and reacts\nto different outcomes in Assembly programming.\nNow, let's discuss memory and addressing modes.\nMemory and addressing modes are key concepts\nin Assembly language that help us understand\nhow data is stored and accessed\nin a computer's memory.\nMemory refers to the physical locations\nwhere data is kept, allowing programs\nto retrieve and manipulate information.\nAddressing modes define the different ways\nwe can access this memory, determining\nhow the CPU interprets the addresses in instructions.\nCommon addressing modes include\nimmediate addressing, direct addressing\nand indirect addressing.\nFor example, in immediate addressing,\nyou might use an instruction like ADD R1, 5,\nwhich adds the value 5 directly to register R1.\nIn direct addressing, the instruction\ncould be ADD R1, [1000], where 1000\nis the memory address containing the value\nto be added to R1.\nLastly, in indirect addressing, you might see\nADD R1, [R2], where register R2 holds the address\nof the value to be added to R1.\nThis demonstrates how addressing modes\nhelp us efficiently access and manage data\nin memory while programming.\nNext, let's take a look at the basic Assembly instructions.\nBasic Assembly instructions fall into several categories,\neach serving a unique function in programming.\nArithmetic instructions perform\nmathematical operations:\nADD R1, R2 adds the value\nin register R2 to R1, while SUB R1, R2\nsubtracts R2 from R1.\nThe MUL and DIV instructions perform\nmultiplication and division, respectively.\nLogic instructions handle bitwise operations:\nAND R1, R2 performs a bitwise AND,\nwhere only bits that are 1 in both R1 and R2 remain 1.\nOR R1, R2 performs a bitwise OR,\nsetting bits to 1 if either register has a 1.\nXOR R1, R2 performs an XOR,\nresulting in 1 if the bits differ.\nThe NOT instruction alternates the bits\nin a register, changing ones to zeroes and vice versa.\nControl instructions direct program flow:\nJMP LABEL causes the program to jump\nto a specific point labeled in the code\nand CMP R1, R2 compares the values in R1 and R2.\nBased on this comparison, JE jumps\nif the values are equal,\nwhile JNE jumps if they are not.\nData movement instructions transfer data:\nMOV R1, R2 moves data from R2 to R1.\nPUSH R1 saves R1's value onto the stack\nand POP R1 retrieves the last value from the stack.\nFinally, bit manipulation instructions include\nSHL which shifts register's bits left,\nessentially multiplying the value by 2,\nand SHR which shifts right, dividing the value by 2.\nROL and ROR rotate bits left or right,\nlooping them back around.\nThese instructions allow precise control over data,\narithmetic and program flow\nin Assembly programming.\nKeep in mind that there are even more\ninstructions available in Assembly,\nproviding further capabilities for developers.\nNow, let's take a look at how Assembly is executed.\nAssembly language is executed through\na process known as the fetch-decode-execute cycle,\nwhich is fundamental to how a CPU operates.\nThis cycle consists of three main stages:\nfetching, decoding and executing instructions.\nDuring the fetch phase, the CPU retrieves\nan instruction from memory, typically pointed\nto by the program counter.\nFor instance, if the instruction is SHL R1,\nthe CPU fetches this command from memory.\nIn the decode phase, the CPU interprets\nthe fetched instruction, determining what action\nis required and identifying the involved operands,\nsuch as recognizing that it needs to shift the bits\nin R1 to the left by one position.\nFinally, in the execute phase, the CPU carries out\nthe operation as specified by the instruction.\nIn our example, it shifts the bits of R1 left,\neffectively multiplying the value by 2.\nThis cycle repeats for each instruction in the program,\nallowing the CPU to process commands sequentially,\nwhether they involve arithmetic calculations,\nlogical operations or control instructions.\nBy understanding this cycle, we see\nhow Assembly language directly translates into actions\nperformed by the computer’s hardware,\nconnecting human-readable code\nto machine-level execution.\nNow that we've learned about the basic instructions\nand how they are executed,\nlet's take a look at a practical code example.\nIn this Assembly code example, we start by loading\nthe values 5 and 10 into registers\nR1 and R2, respectively.\nThe ADD instruction then combines\nthese two values and stores the result in register R3.\nTo determine whether the result is even or odd,\nwe perform a bitwise AND operation\nwith 1 and store the outcome in R4.\nThe AND operation effectively checks\nthe least significant bit of the sum:\nif it’s 0, the number is even;\nif it’s 1, the number is odd.\nInitially, we assume the result is 'Odd'\nby loading this string into register R5.\nWe then compare R4 to 0;\nif they are equal, it indicates that the sum is even\nand the program jumps to the 'even_number' label\nto update R5 with the string 'Even'.\nIf the sum is odd, the program skips this step\nand jumps directly to the 'end' label.\nThis example effectively demonstrates\nbasic arithmetic operations and conditional checks\nin Assembly language.\nNow let's briefly take a look\nat some real-world applications of Assembly.\nAssembly language may seem low-level\ncompared to modern programming languages,\nbut it plays a vital role in many\nreal-world applications where performance\nand precise control over hardware are crucial.\nFor example, it is commonly used\nin embedded systems, like those found in cars,\nmedical devices and household appliances,\nwhere efficiency and direct control\nover hardware resources are essential.\nGame consoles and graphics programming\nalso utilize Assembly for optimizing\nperformance and making the most\nout of hardware capabilities.\nAdditionally, Assembly is critical in device drivers,\nwhich act as the interface\nbetween hardware and the operating system.\nEven parts of operating systems themselves,\nespecially those that need to interact directly\nwith hardware, are often written in Assembly.\nBy using Assembly language, developers can\nfine-tune programs to run as efficiently as possible,\nwhich is particularly important\nin resource-constrained environments\nor performance-critical tasks.\nLastly, let's discuss the limitations of Assembly.\nWhile Assembly language offers significant\nadvantages in terms of performance and control\nover hardware, it also comes with notable limitations.\nOne major drawback is its complexity and verbosity;\nwriting code in Assembly requires a deep\nunderstanding of the underlying hardware architecture,\nwhich can be daunting for many programmers.\nThis complexity leads to longer development times,\nas even simple tasks can require extensive lines\nof code compared to high-level languages.\nAdditionally, Assembly is not portable;\ncode written for one architecture typically cannot\nrun on another without significant modifications.\nThis lack of portability makes Assembly\nless suitable for large-scale software development,\nwhere flexibility and maintainability are essential.\nFurthermore, Assembly lacks the rich libraries\nand built-in features available in high-level languages,\nlimiting its use in modern software applications.\nOverall, while Assembly is powerful for specific tasks,\nits limitations can hinder productivity\nand adaptability in the fast-paced world\nof software development.\nIn conclusion, Assembly language serves\nas a crucial tool for programmers who need\nprecise control over hardware and require\noptimal performance in their applications.\nWhile it offers significant advantages,\nsuch as speed and direct interaction with the CPU,\nit also presents challenges like complexity,\nlack of portability and reduced flexibility\nfor large-scale software development.\nUnderstanding these aspects helps developers\nmake informed decisions about when and how\nto utilize Assembly, ensuring they leverage\nits strengths while being mindful of its limitations.\nThis concludes our exploration\nof the basics of Assembly.\nIf you enjoyed it,\nconsider subscribing to our channel\nand liking the video.\nFeel free to also check out our other videos\non related topics.\nThank you for watching.\n\n## 2. Computer Basics Tutorial\n\nType: doc | text chars: 70516 | page images: 0 | summary chars: 858\n\n### Summary\n\nThis introductory course provides foundational knowledge on computing, explaining that technology profoundly impacts daily life by allowing users to **efficiently input, process, and output information**. The learning material is organised into practical modules that cover core hardware components like **storage, memory, and the central processing unit**, alongside essential security measures and operating systems such as Windows. Learners are also guided through productivity applications, demonstrating how to create and manage documents, presentations, and spreadsheets using **Microsoft Word, PowerPoint, and Excel**. Ultimately, the curriculum is designed to help absolute beginners navigate the digital world responsibly while equipping them with the **essential technical skills** needed to interact safely and productively with modern technology.\n\n### Text\n\nthe exercise files for today's course are located in the video description below don't forget to like and subscribe welcome to Computer Basics where we will learn the fundamentals of computing my name is Mo Jones IT professional and educator this course is for you if you are using a computer for the first time maybe you have not used a computer in quite a while or maybe you're just kind of getting started with using computers we've got you covered in this course this is what we will learn we will first take a look at how technology impacts us today you also learn well what is a computer what's the big fuss about computing why is it so important why does it have such a huge impact on us today and we'll take a look at the ingredients of a computer so what makes up a computer just like a cake we have different ingredients what makes a computer and then we'll take a look at what is software and how do we use it what software is available with a computer if you were to purchase a computer today what software would be available for that particular computer so that's what we'll go ahead and cover today looking to support our channel and get a great deal become a member today to unlock adree videos that's right your favorite courses without a single ad interested in a specific video purchase one of our ad- free courses individually looking for even more gain access to exams certificates and exclusive content at learnit anytime.com more information can be found in the video description below let's answer this big question how does technology impact us today well that is a very broad question and it merits a very very long answer so we'll just focus on the basics here so how does technology impact us today well at the very basic level of technology it basically aids us in performing different tasks so tasks that we would normally you know take a very long time for us to complete technology can aid us to either complete that task more efficiently or faster um or more accurately for example think about handwriting a message and delivering it via mail if you have a friend that's in the next city over it may take some time for that actual message to get to your friend well what if your friend lived in another state or another country uh take a very very long time for that message to get to them but if you use what's called email or electronic mail just typing a message you can send it out travels at the speed of light and your friend will get that message instantly so that's kind of one example of how technology impacts us today now handwriting a message and sending it out versus typing a message and sending it instantly through the means of technology or a computer that's just one example of how technology is impacting us today now there are other ways that technology impacts us um impacts us in a good you know some some positive ways and some some negative ways as well there are some risks with technology at the very least here's what computers do computers they basically store information once the information is stored the information can be processed in the form of answering a question and then the information can be output so if you can think of computers these three concepts right here storing information processing information and outputting information that's pretty much what technology allows us to do on a daily basis now I do have this highlighted here in red some technology will perform physical tasks for us so this is where a lot of discussion happens because obviously there's a lot of positives for this for example sending out you know rescue robots on um you know rescue missions maybe there was a building that collapsed and you can send a robot to go in to search for survivors but then we have other you know just kind of risks with robots going rogue if you will and many other you know automation that can be seen as risky in our environment as well but at the very least this is how technology is impacting us technology is growing very very rapidly and technology is now replacing a lot of things that we would norm normally have you know completed manually now we're seeing cars that are driving themselves and we're seeing drones that are flying themselves as well your refrigerator can let you know when your food is empty and it's time for you to restock so just many different ways that technology impacts us today at the end of the the day the most important thing is that we are being responsible when using technology we want to focus on the positive aspects of technology we want to respect everyone everyone's privacy and to use it for our benefit in continuing our discussion about technology one very popular and common use of technology is the web we usually refer to this as the web or the internet it's officially known as the worldwide web so if you take a look at the URL when you're typing in a message you'll see www at the front that's because it's you're accessing the worldwide web now you may have seen this maybe you taking a peek at your friend's phone or maybe you've picked up a phone and you took a look at you opened up Google or Edge or Firefox or Safari and you notice that you have a web page that allows you to search for information well it's the same concept the web is used by billions of people on a daily basis and the amount of data that's stored on the worldwide web is beyond our comprehension but at the very level it does the same thing information is stored in many different places on actual computers and then information is shaped and readily available or processed in this case maybe we're asking a question so when we're searching for information the question is being run through the computer searches for those results and then it's presented to us now information is accessed by billions globally on a daily basis here's an example of the web so here's a Google Chrome browser very popular browser and we can see it just comes with different elements so up here we can see this is the address bar so this is where we'll type in the name of the website or computer that we want to access down here we have a search bar so down here we can search for something that we're looking for we can even use our microphone to search for something or we can also even upload an image to get a little idea of what it is that we're searching for so I'll just go ahead and just type in a quick question here so I'll say what is computing and when I press enter it's accessing billions and billions and trillions and trillions of of data sets that's been stored and it's automatically kind of filtered through all of that data and returning relevant results for us based on what it's computing and so here we just get a nice little dictionary dictionary definition of what computing is if I scroll down I can get some more examples a lot of different other websites so here's Wikipedia so they have their own series of computers that's storing information and when we access these different websites we're actually accessing information that's being stored on those computers right here's some other ones here from universities from organizations from dictionaries we even had videos in here explaining what technology is or computing is so a lot of different things but this is just a great example of how we can put that into practice we're accessing data that's already stored when we ask a question that data is being processed and then we're provided with the output so we're accessing the same data that billions of people worldwide are able to access so if you were to go to another country and type in what is computing you get a lot of these similar results here maybe in a different language based on the country that you're in but for the most part all of these sources are coming back for us in real time so I think the main thing to know about here about the worldwide web is that to access the worldwide web you need a browser right so that's kind of the first part you need a web browser to be able to access the worldwide web the worldwide web comes with either a address bar here where we can actually type in the actual address that we're looking for this could be something like youtube.com or amazon.com and then it also comes with a search feature so those are the basics right we could search for info so those are the basics with the worldwide web or the web or maybe you want to call it the internet or maybe you just want to call it the net it's all pretty much the same thing data is input we're processing the information and the information is readily made available for us what is a computer well I just used a computer to access information on the worldwide web i was able to open up a web browser and access data that is stored on other computers on the worldwide web but what exactly is a computer yes it is a physical device but before we take a look at the physical aspects of a computer let's remember the logic of a computer and that it does three basic things it allows us to input information it allows us or it performs processing of that information for us and it also outputs that information so input process output when you're using your phone which is an actual computer as well because it goes through the same process you're inputting information maybe you're asking a question from an assistant like Google Assistant or Siri and your phone is going to process that information it takes the voice command that you gave it goes through its huge data storage finds what you're looking for and provides you with the information that you're looking for and a means of output either by displaying the information on your screen or by giving you some some audio as well so that's what a computer is now we do have different components of a computer we have hardware which are the physical components of a computer we'll take a look at that on the next slide and we have software so we have hardware but we need need some type of software in order for them to kind of talk to each other and then we also have some type of security we want to make sure that we're the data that we're accessing is always available and that it's it always maintains its integrity let's take a look at hardware now I opened up the back of an older laptop that I had laying around and this is kind of what it looks like in the back so you'll see the orange part there that's the storage so that is the hard drive or the hard disk the purple that's the memory so the memory helps in processing the information that we are requesting and then the blue this little card right here this is an actual network card or a wireless card right so this is what allows us to connect to a well really to to connect to the web and then finally we have another one which is called the CPU now the CPU typically we don't have access to this i would need to remove about 15 more screws remove this plastic panel so that I can get to the CPU the CPU is the central processing unit it's a very tiny um device and it processes millions of in of instructions per per second so these are the different components now we don't have to worry about them they're you know they're securely enclosed in the back of our phone or our tablet or our computer and they just work so they do all the work in the background but I just wanted to show us just kind of the different components these are the basic components here right so again here's our hard drive or our hard disk they kind of look like this now so the newer hard drives they kind of look like this they are pretty thin look like a little card and then of course we have our connectivity and there's a lot of other components on here such as other places different slots that you can plug things into we can see we have a speaker right here we have microphones keyboards a lot of different aspects of it but for the most part these are the main components storage memory need to be able to connect to the network and it needs to be able to process the information so we can see all of those elements are right in here so remember we talked about inputting information so the input the information is stored on the hard disk and then the CPU processes the information for us and typically the network can be used to output the information for us as well and of course memory is aiding in all of these different options here as well so that's a quick rundown with how computers work what is a computer what are the different components of a computer when we come back we'll take a look at the other aspects which is the software so we'll take a look at the software components and the security components as well the other components of a computer system is software and security now software is another word or another way of just saying apps for daily computer operators we typically use the phrase apps sometimes we can even call it software applications for example if we wanted to use electronic mail here's a application or software application called Outlook remember we discussed earlier about writing a message electronically and sending it to our friend in another city state or country and they'll receive that message at the speed of light that is what that allows us to do so all these different software applications they allow us to do different things if we wanted to access the worldwide web we can use Microsoft Edge or Google Chrome and we can type in the address that we're looking for or we can go ahead and search for information as well we have the clock so different time zones tells the time we have alarms we have timers on there if we want to go ahead and create a document such as a flyer or a resume we can use Microsoft Word for that we'll cover that a little later if we want to create presentations so we have what's called PowerPoint so we can create slides and communicate messages clearly to our audience and then of course for more spreadsheet and number crunching and data collection we have Microsoft Excel we'll cover these a little later the other aspect of computing is security in this case we'll focus on the Windows security system when we take a look at the Windows computer here now the main thing with security we want to make sure that all these applications that we are accessing that they are doing what they're supposed to do that they're providing us with the correct information and that they're sending the information exactly where we say say it should go so security will keep an eye on these applications make sure that they're safe for us to use make sure that you know your data is not being stolen or whatever you're inputting is not being spied on so that's what security is for security covers more than just software it also protects the hardware on your computer and all the components of computing as well so between hardware software and security we have a fully functional computer system that allows us to interact with it use it safely and interact with data that is authentic and applications that work exactly as they are supposed to work now let's take a look at operating systems now operating systems is a type of software but it's the primary software for a computer now if you walked into a store you may have recognized or you may recognize some of these icons here these are different operating systems so if you're picking up a computer you may see this icon if you're picking up a phone you may see this icon or you if you're picking up another type of computer if you're picking up something like a Chromebook you may see this icon here so these are different operating systems and they're used for laptops phones and tablets so every phone laptop or tablet that you use they're using some type of operating system some are simple some are more complex and some offer more features or less than others and at the end of the day you're just going to go ahead and choose which operating system works best for you so what do all these mean well this is a this is the Windows operating system this is the Chrome so if you get a Chromebook you'll get the Chrome operating system and here's Mac so this is Mac the Mac operating system as well let's take a deeper dive into operating systems now what does an operating system do now an operating system pretty much glues everything together so it control it controls all the operations of a computer system we need something to make the hardware talk to the software and the applications and the security and we need some type of way to interact and request information and view information so the operating system provides a graphical user interface so instead of just looking at code you know ones and zeros and X's and O's we're actually seeing pictures on our screen boxes and different shapes that we can interact with a folder icon looks just like a folder right a video icon just looks like a video camera operating systems have a file and folder structure whether you're using a phone laptop or tablet there is a file and folder structure you need to be able to access files and folders of course it also controls the security so the operating system um will need some type of security to make sure that it's up and running the operating system can keep an eye on the security system and the security system can keep an eye on the operating system and of course we can access the apps through the operating system as well so in a nutshell that's what the operating system will allow us to to do we we are going to take a look at the Windows operating system and we'll spend most of our time there seeing how we can take advantage of the graphical user interface the file and folder structure the applications and the security as well let's get started so the first thing we need to do is to locate the power button and turn on the computer so if you're using a laptop you should see button typically on the top left or the top right of the actual laptop this one is kind of in the middle you click on the button that turns on the laptop if you're using something that looks like this this is a desktop computer cuz it fits on your desktop it's type of big and clunky and but it's very very powerful typically they're more powerful than laptops just click on the power button and that will allow you to be up and running now once you click on that power button you should be pre presented with some type of startup but in this case you'll be presented with a screen that looks like this again this is