ng Devices and Explain\nIn manual manufacturing, locking devices are essential for ensuring safety and preventing accidental operation of machinery. Here are some common locking devices and their applications:\nLock Nuts: Designed to resist loosening under vibrations and torque, commonly used in machinery, automotive, and construction industries.\nLock Washers: Prevent nuts and bolts from coming loose by exerting a load and flexing to keep the nut in place.\nThread-locking Fluids: Adhesive applied to the threads of fasteners to fill gaps and harden, preventing loosening in various applications.\nSplit Pins: Simple devices that prevent a nut from unscrewing from a bolt by inserting into a hole drilled through the bolt.\nRetaining Compounds: Adhesive used to secure bearings, bushings, and cylindrical parts into housings or onto shafts.\nThese devices are crucial in maintaining safety standards and preventing accidents in manufacturing environments. They ensure that machinery remains secure and that workers are protected from potential hazards.\n\n## 3. NC1780 - MANUAL MANUFACTURING L2 QP NOV 2013 NEW.pdf\n\nType: pdf | text chars: 5788 | page images: 18 | summary chars: 612\n\n### Summary\n\nThis document outlines an **assessment for a technical education course** focused on manual manufacturing at the vocational level. It provides students with clear directives, including the **requirement to answer all questions** and use appropriate drawing utensils for graphical tasks. The evaluation tests practical competencies by asking learners to **identify specialized workshop tools** and analyze technical diagrams. Additionally, it emphasizes workplace safety and protocols by including scenarios that require students to **complete formal accident reports** and understand proper workshop maintenance.\n\n### Text\n\nMANUAL MANUFACTURING NQF LEVEL 2\nNOVEMBER EXAMINATION\n(6030092)\n15 November 2013 (X-Paper) 09:00–12:00\nThis question paper consists of 8 pages and 1 addendum.\