histicated safety features in vehicles and other applications. Smarter Systems: Development of more intelligent and adaptive systems capable of learning and responding to their environment. Greater Connectivity: Expanded use of networked mechatronic systems, creating opportunities for remote monitoring and control. ```\n20260209_143944.jpg\nExtracted Text:\nNo text.\nAnalysis:\nNo analysis available.\n20260209_144013.jpg\nExtracted Text:\nDisadvantages of Mechatronics System 1. The initial cost is very high 2. The complicated design and system 3. The repair and maintenance is complex. X 4. its replacement is difficult, that it is difficult to change old system to new system. 5. Imperative to have Knowledge of different engineering fields for design and implementation. 6. Specific problem of various systems will have to be addressed separately and properly Sh Sikap forears 600\nAnalysis:\n```html Summary The provided text outlines several disadvantages associated with mechatronics systems. These disadvantages range from high initial costs and complex designs to challenges in maintenance, replacement, and the need for multidisciplinary expertise. The text also highlights the need to address specific problems within various subsystems individually. There are also some extraneous, non-relevant characters and phrases at the end that appear to be noise. Key Points High Initial Cost: Implementing mechatronics systems requires a significant upfront investment. Complex Design and System: Mechatronics systems are inherently complex, making their design challenging. Complex Repair and Maintenance: Due to their intricate nature, repairs and maintenance can be difficult and costly. Difficult Replacement: Upgrading or replacing older mechatronics systems with newer ones can be a complicated process. Multidisciplinary Knowledge Required: Designing and implementing mechatronics systems necessitates expertise in various engineering fields. System-Specific Problem Solving: Addressing