 IEC standard. ALL terminal numbers must be labelled. Write neatly and legibly.\n(6040213) -3- NC2100(E)(M11)V\nQUESTION 1 1.1 Study FIGURE 1 showing an electronic-controlled glue-heating system that\nfeeds the end effecter (glue gun) of an industrial robot system.\nFIGURE 1\nDraw the systems diagram that will represent the glue-heating subsystem. (10) 1.2 Study FIGURE 2 showing an electronic heating control circuit.\nFIGURE 2\n1.2.1 Name the components A, C and F. (3) 1.2.2 Explain the operation of component A. (2)\nElectronic box\nHeater Start\nStop Run\nStandby\nA\nB\nC\nD\nE\nF\nRv Re\nQ1\nH1\n240 V\n6 V\nR1\nRt D1\n-t\n(6040213) -4- NC2100(E)(M11)V\n1.2.3 Explain how component B is turned on. (2) 1.2.4 Explain how the electronic heating control circuit operates to\nmaintain the glue at a constant temperature.\n(3) [20] QUESTION 2 2.1 Study FIGURE 3 showing three motors represented by A, B and C.\nFIGURE 3\nIdentify the THREE motors represented by A, B and C. (3) 2.2 Explain the difference between a DC motor and a servo-motor. (2 + 2) (4) 2.3 Draw a labelled construction of a stepper motor. (4) 2.4 Explain the purpose of a servo-motor in mechatronic applications. (2) 2.5 Which semiconductor component is used in phase-speed control? (1) 2.6 Explain, with the aid of a drawing, how the speed of a DC motor can be\ncontrolled manually by using resistors.\n(6) [20]\nA B C\n(6040213) -5- NC2100(E)(M11)V\nQUESTION 3 3.1 Describe the operation of a positive-displacement pump. (3) 3.2 Briefly explain how a pressure-relief valve is used for safety on a hydraulic\npump.\n(4) 3.3 A hydraulic accumulator is a pressure vessel used for storing fluid under\npressure. List THREE types of accumulators.\n(3)\n3.4 The furnace door shown in FIGURE 4 is opened and closed by a\ndouble-acting cylinder. The cylinder is to be actuated by a spring-return 4/2-way valve. This ensures that the door opens only as long as the valve is actuated. When the valve actuating lever is released the door closes again.\nFIGURE 4\nDraw the hydraulic circuit diagram for this application showing the pressure\nparts.\n(11) [21]\n(6040213) -6- NC2100(E)(M11)V\nQUESTION 4 4.1 Poppet valves and piston slide-valves differ in design.\nGive FOUR advantages of using a poppet valve.\n(4)\n4.2 Directional-control valves (DCV) are classified according to the number of\nports. Study FIGURE 5 showing the symbol for an electrohydraulic directional-control valve.\nFIGURE 5\nList FIVE characteristics of the valve shown in FIGURE 5. (5) 4.3 Description of operation:\nA sorting device shown in FIGURE 6 is used to sort heavy steel workpieces. When a start push button is pressed the piston rod of a double-acting cylinder pushes the adjacent workpiece off the conveyor belt. When the start push button is released the piston rod returns to its retracted end position at a slow adjustable speed. Positional sketch:\nFIGURE 6\nDesign the working and electrical control circuits that will comply with ALL\nmentioned operational conditions. (6 + 6)\n(12) [21]\n(6040213) -7- NC2100(E)(M11)V\nQUESTION 5 5.1 Give FOUR causes of low or erratic oil pressure. (4) 5.2 List FOUR precautions to check before restarting a hydraulic subsystem after\nit has been repaired.\n(4) 5.3 Fault-finding is often performed in a random and haphazard manner. There\nare various levels of maintenance concerned with getting a faulty plant running again, like repair of complete units and the return of equipment to the manufacturer for repair. Draw a flow chart that represents fault recording and the fault-analysis process.\n(10)\n[18]\nTOTAL: 100\n\n## 4. Pasted text\n\nType: doc | text chars: 13492 | page images: 0 | summary chars: 794\n\n### Summary\n\nThis provided document serves as an **advanced mechatronic systems examination paper and memorandum**, structured to evaluate a student's mastery of both theoretical engineering concepts and practical workshop applications. The text is methodically organized into distinct modules covering **technical systems architecture**, **relay and electronic control logic**, **industrial computing**, and **fluid power actuation**. Its primary educational purpose is to test a candidate's progression from foundational vocabulary and mechanical calculations to complex **system troubleshooting and workplace safety protocols**. Ultimately, it functions as a comprehensive diagnostic tool that bridges the gap between **theoretical design principles** and the safe execution of **industrial automation**.\n\n### Text\n\nThis is a comprehensive, advanced study guide designed to master the core principles of Mechatronic Systems, aligning with the learning outcomes and typical examination structures for the March assessments. Approaching mechatronics requires a systems-level architectural mindset—very similar to full-stack development. You are dealing with discrete layers (mechanical, electrical, electronic, and computational) that must communicate seamlessly through defined protocols and logic gates. Here is your master study guide, synthesized from your syllabus outlines and past examination standards. Module 1: Technical Systems Architecture & Energy Flow This module forms the foundation of understanding how physical systems process inputs into useful outputs. 1.1 Defining the Technical System A technical system is defined by its ability to process three core variables: energy, information, and matter.\nSystem Hierarchy: You must be able to break down a complex system into sub-systems and individual components.\nEnergy Conversion: Energy flow is represented using functional block diagrams that map the conversion of energy through different stages (e.g., a hydro-electric plant converting kinetic water energy into electrical energy). 1.2 Applied Mechanical Calculations Examinations frequently test your ability to calculate the mechanical work done within these systems.\nWork & Energy: To calculate the minimum work done by a system (like a winch lifting a cage), use the physical formula for work against gravity: W = m \\cdot g \\cdot h, where m is mass, g is gravitational acceleration, and h is distance.\nTransmission Speeds: Be prepared to perform speed calculations for mechanical couplings, specifically gear trains and belt drives. Module 2: Basic Control Circuits & Logic This section translates physical requirements into logical control structures, moving from hardwired relays to electronic boards. 2.1 Relay Ladder-Logic Relay circuits form the backbone of hardwired industrial control.\nEnabling Conditions: Control circuits rely on specific conditions to change state. You must understand the difference between event-based enabling conditions (e.g., a limit switch being physically triggered) and time-based enabling conditions (e.g., a delay timer reaching its preset value).\nCascaded Control: Understand how to design a cascaded timer-based control circuit, where multiple outputs (like indicator lamps) trigger in a positive sequence with precise intervals (e.g., 5-second delays). 2.2 Electronic Control Components Modern systems utilize solid-state electronics for finer control. You must be able to identify and explain the operation of components within a circuit diagram (such as a glue-heating system for an industrial robot).\nThermistors (NTC/PTC): Used to monitor and maintain constant temperatures.\nTransistors & Diodes: Understand how a transistor operates as an electronic switch to energize a relay coil, and the protective role of diodes. Module 3: Industrial Computing & Automation The computational layer. This is where hardware meets software execution. 3.1 The Role of Computers in Industry Understand the distinct roles computers play in modern manufacturing and design processes.\nDesign & Manufacturing: Differentiate between CAD (Computer-Aided Design), CAM (Computer-Aided Manufacturing), and the overarching CIM (Computer-Integrated Manufacturing) systems.\nFMS: Describe Flexible Manufacturing Systems and how intelligent devices allow production lines to adapt dynamically. 3.2 Programmable Logic Controllers (PLCs)\nBe able to explain the construction and function of a PLC.\nUnderstand how to map traditional ladder logic into PLC software elements and connect them safely in accordance with regulations. Module 4: Actuation (Electro-Pneumatics & Motors) The execution layer. How systems enact physical change. 4.1 Electric Motors Understand the operational differences and specific use-cases for various industrial motors.\nDC Motors vs. Servo-Motors: Be able to explain their differences, particularly how servo-motors are utilized for high-precision mechatronic applications (positioning and feedback).\nStepper Motors: Understand their physical construction and how they allow for discrete angular movements.\nControl: Grasp how phase-speed control is achieved using semiconductor components, and how manual speed control is achieved using resistor networks. 4.2 Fluid Power (Pneumatics & Hydraulics) Fluid power relies heavily on standardized IEC symbols.\nPneumatic Service Units: Be able to draw and label the components of a basic air preparation unit (Filter, Regulator, Lubricator).\nDirectional Control Valves (DCV): Understand the construction, symbols, and operation of solenoid valves (e.g., a single-acting 3/2 solenoid spring return valve). Know the advantages of poppet valves versus piston slide-valves.\nHydraulics: Explain the operation of positive-displacement pumps, the safety functions of pressure-relief valves, and the purpose of hydraulic accumulators (pressure storage vessels). Module 5: Troubleshooting & System Safety Protocols for debugging and maintaining hardware. 5.1 Diagnostic Methodology Fault-finding must be systematic, not random.\nTesting Protocols: Discuss the critical differences, particularly regarding personal safety, between live-circuit testing and dead-circuit testing.\nFlow Charts: Be prepared to draw logical fault-finding flow charts used to troubleshoot control circuits and analyze faults. 5.2 Commissioning and Maintenance\nList the standard commissioning checks performed before launching a new electropneumatic application.\nIdentify causes of system failure (e.g., low or erratic oil pressure in hydraulics) and list safety precautions to verify before restarting a repaired subsystem. Would you like me to generate a practice quiz focused specifically on the IEC pneumatic symbols and block diagram architectures to test your readiness?\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHere is a comprehensive, advanced-level March Control Test and Memorandum designed to evaluate proficiency in Mechatronic Systems, synthesizing principles from both Level 2 and Level 3 frameworks. NATIONAL CERTIFICATE (VOCATIONAL) - MARCH CONTROL TEST SUBJECT: MECHATRONIC SYSTEMS LEVEL: NQF LEVEL 2 & 3 HYBRID EVALUATION TIME: 1 HOUR MARKS: 40 INSTRUCTIONS:\nAnswer ALL the questions.\nNumber the answers correctly according to the numbering system used in this paper.\nShow all mathematical calculations and formulas where applicable.\nAssume g = 9.81 m/s² for all gravitational calculations. QUESTION 1: TECHNICAL SYSTEMS & ENERGY CONVERSION [10 MARKS] 1.1 Define a technical system by identifying the three primary variables that flow through it. (3) 1.2 A mechatronic lifting mechanism (winch) is installed to hoist a replacement robotic arm. The arm has a mass of 450 kg and must be lifted vertically from the ground floor to a mezzanine platform 12 m above. Calculate the minimum amount of work done by the winch to achieve this. (4) 1.3 In the context of computer-integrated environments, explain the difference between CAD and CAM. (3) QUESTION 2: CONTROL CIRCUITS & INTELLIGENT DEVICES [13 MARKS] 2.1 Relay ladder-logic control circuits rely on specific conditions to execute automated sequences. Differentiate between event-based enabling conditions and time-based enabling conditions. (4) 2.2 A temperature control circuit uses a thermistor to monitor the heat of an industrial glue-dispenser. Briefly explain the operation of a Negative Temperature Coefficient (NTC) thermistor within this circuit. (3) 2.3 Explain how a transistor operates as a switching component to energize a relay coil in an electronic heating control circuit. (3) 2.4 List three types of information that can be processed or found within technical systems. (3) QUESTION 3: ACTUATION (FLUID POWER & MOTORS) [10 MARKS] 3.1 In electropneumatic and electrohydraulic systems, directional-control valves direct the flow of fluid. State two operational advantages of using a poppet valve over a piston slide-valve. (2) 3.2 A hydraulic accumulator is classified as a pressure vessel for storing fluid under pressure. List three distinct types of hydraulic accumulators. (3) 3.3 While designing the end-effector for a sorting device, an engineer must choose an actuator. Explain the primary functional difference between a standard DC motor and a servo-motor in this application. (4) 3.4 Identify the semiconductor component most commonly utilized for phase-speed control in motor applications. (1) QUESTION 4: TROUBLESHOOTING & SAFETY [7 MARKS] 4.1 When fault-finding the control circuit of an electropneumatic application, technicians must adhere to strict safety protocols. Discuss the fundamental difference between live-circuit testing and dead-circuit testing concerning personal safety. (4) 4.2 List three critical precautions that must be checked before restarting a hydraulic subsystem after a repair has been completed. (3) TOTAL: 40 MARKS MEMORANDUM / MARKING GUIDELINES QUESTION 1 1.1 A technical system is defined by the flow and processing of:\nEnergy (1)\nInformation (1)\nMatter (1) 1.2 Calculation for work done:\nFormula: W = m \\cdot g \\cdot h (1)\nSubstitution: W = 450 \\cdot 9.81 \\cdot 12 (1)\nSolution: W = 52974 (1)\nUnit: Joules / J (1) 1.3 CAD vs. CAM:\nCAD (Computer-Aided Design): The use of computer software to create, modify, analyze, or optimize physical designs and technical drawings. (1.5)\nCAM (Computer-Aided Manufacturing): The use of software to control machine tools and related machinery in the manufacturing of workpieces designed in CAD. (1.5) QUESTION 2 2.1 Enabling conditions:\nEvent-based: The system shifts states based on a physical occurrence or trigger, such as a limit switch being pressed by a moving part, or a sensor detecting a workpiece. (2)\nTime-based: The system shifts states based on an internal clock or timer delay (e.g., a delay-on timer), regardless of physical spatial events. (2) 2.2 NTC Thermistor Operation:\nAs the ambient temperature of the glue/heater increases, the electrical resistance of the NTC thermistor decreases. (2)\nThis change in resistance alters the voltage/current in the control circuit to regulate the heating elements. (1) 2.3 Transistor switching:\nA small control current applied to the base of the transistor allows a much larger current to flow from the collector to the emitter. (2)\nThis larger current is what energizes the heavier relay coil, which then switches the main power circuit. (1) 2.4 Types of information in systems (Any 3):\nControl signals (1)\nFeedback data (1)\nProcessing status/Parameters (1) (Accept reasonable equivalents: Input instructions, sensor data, display outputs). QUESTION 3 3.1 Advantages of a poppet valve (Any 2):\nHighly leak-proof / better sealing due to the seating design. (1)\nLess susceptible to wear from dirt/particles compared to slide valves. (1)\nFaster response times / shorter strokes. (1) 3.2 Types of accumulators:\nBladder accumulator (1)\nDiaphragm accumulator (1)\nPiston accumulator (1) 3.3 DC Motor vs. Servo-Motor:\nDC Motor: Provides continuous rotation and speed control, generally running in open-loop systems without precise positional awareness. (2)\nServo-Motor: Features an integrated feedback mechanism (encoder/resolver) allowing for highly precise closed-loop control of angular position, velocity, and acceleration. (2) 3.4 Phase-speed control component:\nThyristor (or Triac). (1) QUESTION 4 4.1 Live vs. Dead testing:\nLive-circuit testing: Taking voltage/current readings while the machine is powered on. High risk of electrical shock and requires specialized PPE and insulated tools. (2)\nDead-circuit testing: Done with the machine isolated from the power source (Lockout/Tagout). Generally safer; involves testing for continuity and resistance. (2) 4.2 Precautions before restarting (Any 3):\nEnsure all mechanical guards and safety covers are securely reinstalled. (1)\nVerify fluid levels are correct and no leaks are present at repaired joints. (1)\nEnsure all tools, rags, and repair equipment are cleared from the moving parts area. (1)\nBleed trapped air from the hydraulic lines before full pressurization. (1) DIAGNOSTIC ANALYSIS OF THE ASSESSMENT This Question Paper is engineered to evaluate candidates across multiple cognitive levels, transitioning from rote recall to applied engineering logic.\nRecall & Recognition (Approx. 30%): Questions 1.1, 2.4, 3.1, 3.2, and 3.4 test foundational vocabulary and component identification. This ensures the student has internalized the \"language\" of mechatronics.\nUnderstanding & Explanation (Approx. 40%): Questions 1.3, 2.1, 2.2, 2.3, 3.3, and 4.1 require the student to articulate how and why systems operate. The heavy focus here is on the transition from physical logic (relays) to solid-state logic (transistors/thermistors) and the critical distinction between open and closed-loop actuation (Motors).\nApplication & Calculation (Approx. 15%): Question 1.2 isolates mechanical physics principles, verifying the student can quantify the physical demands of an automated system.\nSynthesis & Safety Protocols (Approx. 15%): Question 4.2 bridges the gap between theoretical understanding and practical, real-world workshop environments where systematic safety is paramount. Would you like me to draft a secondary assessment that focuses purely on designing and drawing the actual electrical and pneumatic circuit diagrams?\n\n"
