      "excerpt": "NC (V) Mechatronics Level 2 Integrated Summative Assessment Task source capture Notebook: https://notebook.google.com/notebook/4c380566 5aad 473a a99c 2d3045ceaf2d Captured: 2026 09 30T21:02:53.805Z | walker: notebooklm ingestion extension | sources: 3/3 | text chars: 16917 | pag",
      "content": "# NC (V) Mechatronics Level 2 Integrated Summative Assessment Task - source capture\n\nNotebook: https://notebook.google.com/notebook/4c380566-5aad-473a-a99c-2d3045ceaf2d\nCaptured: 2026-09-30T21:02:53.805Z | walker: notebooklm-ingestion extension | sources: 3/3 | text chars: 16917 | page images: 0 | summaries: 2\nProvenance: research/captures/notebooklm_sources/05_NC_V_Mechatronics_Level_2_Integrated_Summative_Assessment_Task_4c380566.json sha256 D257B01BD2B01FD19FCF3EC31A460BD0DADFFA988792B7E3BCEBAE35A67B5B8F\n\n## 1. arrow_drop_down\n\nType: doc | text chars: 0 | page images: 0 | summary chars: 0\n\n_No extractable text or page images captured._\n\n## 2. G Clamp Manufacturing Activity Questions\n\nType: doc | text chars: 4203 | page images: 0 | summary chars: 536\n\n### Summary\n\nThe provided text outlines a practical guide for **crafting a G-clamp**, detailing every sequential phase from initial layout to final assembly. It establishes essential **safety precautions and tool requirements** to ensure that all cutting, drilling, and threading procedures are performed securely and accurately. Furthermore, the document highlights the technical reasoning behind specific engineering practices, such as **lead-in chamfers and force-fit assemblies**, ensuring the finished tool can withstand heavy industrial usage.\n\n### Text\n\nTimestamp: 2026-08-21 05:43:45\nUser: From the document ISAT answer all the Activity Questions regarding the Manual Manufacturing part. Manufacturering the g clamp.\nGemini: Sub-task 3: Manufacture a Work Piece (G-Clamp) (Total: 50 Marks; 240 Minutes)[cite: 2]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nActivity 3.1: Planning for the Manufacturing Task (Worksheet 6)\nFive Key Work Sequence Processes:\nPreparation and Marking: Measure, scribe layout lines, and center-punch reference points on the material according to the engineering drawing specifications[cite: 1, 2].\nMaterial Removal & Squaring: Cut away excess waste material, file surfaces flat and square to the reference marks, and deburr all edges to eliminate sharp points[cite: 1, 2].\nPrecision Drilling: Prepare the drill press, clamp the work piece securely, and drill the main frame hole for the screw thread as well as the ends of the screw handle[cite: 1, 2].\nInternal Thread Fabrication: Tap the main frame hole to the specified thread size and apply a lead-in chamfer for smooth engagement[cite: 1, 2].\nAssembly & Finishing: Perform a force-fit assembly of the handle and washer onto the screw thread, complete final fitting, clean tools, and tidy the workstation[cite: 1, 2].\nTool Requirements:\nMarking & Layout: Steel rule, scriber, engineer's square, center punch, and ball-peen hammer[cite: 1, 2].\nCutting & Shaping: Hacksaw, flat hand files, square files, and deburring tools[cite: 1, 2].\nDrilling & Threading: Drill press, machine vice/clamps, twist drill bits, tap set with tap wrench, and chamfering countersink bit[cite: 1, 2].\nSafety Requirements:\nSafety goggles are mandatory during all drilling and material cutting operations to protect against high-velocity metal swarf and chips[cite: 1, 2].\nEnsure work pieces are clamped securely in vices before starting any drilling or cutting operation[cite: 1, 2].\nMaintain clean, hazard-free walkways and keep hands clear of moving machinery[cite: 1, 2].\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nActivity 3.2: Manufacturing the Work Piece\nMeasuring, Scribing, and Center Punching: Verify stock dimensions against the drawing, scribe all reference lines, and mark hole centers with a center punch prior to cutting or drilling[cite: 1, 2].\nRough Cutting and Filing: Cut away excess stock with a hacksaw, file all faces flat and square to reference marks, and deburr all edges[cite: 1, 2].\nDrilling Operations: Securely mount the work piece on the drill press and drill the primary screw-thread clearance/tap hole and screw-handle end holes to exact dimensions[cite: 1, 2].\nTapping and Chamfering: Apply a lead-in chamfer to the drilled hole to prevent thread distortion and tap the frame hole to the specified thread size using cutting fluid[cite: 1, 2].\nForce-Fit Assembly: Assemble the screw-thread handle at one end and the washer at the other end using a high-precision interference force fit to withstand operational clamping torque[cite: 1, 2].\nHousekeeping & Storage: Deburr finished edges, clean all hand and power tools, return tools to the store, and clear the work area[cite: 1, 2].\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nActivity 3.3: Knowledge Questionnaire (Worksheet 7)\nQuestion 1 — Purpose of the Lead-in Chamfer: Chamfering the tapped hole creates a tapered entry that facilitates smooth thread engagement, prevents cross-threading, and protects the entrance threads from mechanical damage during heavy clamping loads[cite: 1].\nQuestion 2 — Mechanics of the Force-Fit Assembly: The handle and washer utilize high-precision interference fit tolerances to create a permanent mechanical bond capable of resisting rotational and axial torque during industrial clamping without slippage[cite: 1].\nQuestion 3 — Occupational Health, Safety, and Housekeeping (OHS): Mandatory use of eye protection (safety goggles) prevents ocular injuries from metal swarf[cite: 1, 2]. Immediate cleaning and returning of tools to designated storage prevents workspace clutter, minimizes trip hazards, and protects precision measuring faces from degradation[cite: 1, 2].\n\n## 3. ElectroAndIntro-ISAT.pdf\n\nType: pdf | text chars: 12714 | page images: 0 | summary chars: 771\n\n### Summary\n\nThis document outlines the **National Certificate Level 2 assessment guidelines for mechatronics students**, serving as a comprehensive manual for their **practical examination**. The evaluation is divided into **three core sub-tasks** designed to test diverse technical abilities, including **computer setup and programmable logic controller programming**, **electronic circuit wiring and board soldering**, and the **mechanical fabrication of a G-clamp**. Furthermore, students must complete **associated knowledge questionnaires** and adhere strictly to workshop safety protocols throughout every phase. Ultimately, the assessment provides a **structured marking framework** to accurately measure each candidate's vocational competence and practical skill acquisition.\n\n### Text\n\nhigher education & training\nDepartment: Higher Education and Training REPUBLIC OF SOUTH AFRICA\nNATIONAL CERTIFICATE (VOCATIONAL)\nNQF LEVEL 2\nREVIEWED INTEGRATED SUMMATIVE ASSESSMENT TASK\nPROGRAMME ISAT\nINSTRUCTIONS TO STUDENTS\nPROGRAMME: MECHATRONICS LEVEL 2\nSUBJECT CODES: 10040052; 12040212; 06030092\nDATE OF IMPLEMENTATION: 01 JANUARY 2020\nThis document consists of 8 pages.\nCopyright reserved\nPlease tum ever\nNC (V) MECHATRONICS 22\nThis is a practical application task that tests your ability to apply the knowledge and skills acquired in the vocational subject. The work required from you is an important and compulsory component\nof your studies.\n1.\n1.1\n1.2\n1.3\nGeneral information\nStudy the task carefully.\nNote the mark and time allocations per sub-task and for the task as a whole. Note the assessment date(s).\n1.4\nStudy the resources required carefully.\n1.5 Use the assessment tool as guidance on how your work will be marked. 1.6 You are required to provide proof that you submitted work done by yourself.\n2.\n3.\nTask description\nThe ISAT consists of three sub-tasks. For sub-task 1, you must write and simulate a PLC program and assemble a personal computer. For sub-task 2, you must design and wire an electronic circuit and for sub-task 3, you must analyse a mechanical drawing and manufacture the drawn work piece.\nAll three sub-tasks require worksheets to be completed. The worksheets will be handed to you on the assessment days only. Make sure that you have a pen and pencil to complete the worksheets and that you hand in the completed worksheets for marking.\nInstructions for the task\n3.1 Sub-task 1: Design a PLC program and assemble a computer\n(Total: 50 marks; 180 minutes)\nActivity 1.1: Identify computer peripherals (Sub-total: 6 marks; 15 minutes)\nDuring a supervised session, you must use a pen to complete Worksheet 1, handed out at the beginning of the session only.\nActivity 1.2: Connect the peripherals to the computer (Sub-total: 9 marks; 45 minutes)\nYour lecturer will give you instructions to connect the different peripherals identified in activity 1.1 to the computer tower and boot up the computer.\nActivity 1.3: Produce a Word document and complete a PLC wiring diagram (Sub-total: 15 marks; 50 minutes)\nBrief: A normally open pushbutton (start) is connected to input 1 of the PLC, a normally closed pushbutton (stop) is connected to input 2 and relay K1 is connected to output 1 of the PLC. You must switch the power to the PLC on and off by means of an on/off detent switch.\nOperation: When the start pushbutton is pressed, output 1 will energise and latch through an auxiliary contact of output 1 and stay on. An on-delay timer will be energised at the same time. The output will stay on for ten seconds and switch off. The output can also be de- energised by pressing the stop pushbutton input 2 (stop).\nCopyright reserved\n2\nPlease turn over\nCS 2 202\ned\nNC (V) MECHATRONICS LEVEL 2 2020\nCreate a material list:\nCreate a new document in MS Word.\nIn the header of this document, put your NAME, STUDENT NUMBER and the DATE.\nIn your document, create a Table for the materials list for this brief.\nUse the example in Figure 1 to draw up the table.\n• Use the following specifications:\no the first row of the table must be double spacing;\no the heading must be centred;\no all header text must be Arial 12 bold.\nFill in the missing information on your materials list.\nThe text must be in Italic.\nSave your work on a flash drive.\n• Print a copy of your document.\n\t\nMaterial List\n\t\t\n\nInput's\nItem\n\t\nDescription\n\t\nMarking\n\t\nAddress\n\n1\n\t\t\t\n\n2\n\t\t\t\n\n3\n\t\t\t\n\nOutputs\n\t\t\t\n\n4\n\t\t\t\n\n5\n\t\t\t\n\nPLC Unit\n\t\t\t\n\nSpecial Relays\n\t\t\t\nFIGURE 1\nComplete a wiring diagram:\nYour lecturer will hand out Worksheet 2. Complete the PLC wiring diagram and hand in the completed worksheet for marking.\nActivity 1.4: Creating a PLC source program and simulate program\n(Sub-total: 20 marks; 80 minutes)\nBrief to students: When 11 (START) is activated, Q1 will be ON and latched through a latching contact of Q1. Simultaneously a timer will be activated for 10 seconds. After the 10 seconds have elapsed, Q1 must switch OFF. Q1 can also be switched OFF when 12 (STOP) is activated any time before the 10 seconds elapsed.\nCopyright reserved.\n184\n11\n2\nPlease turn over\nNCM MECHATRONICS LEVEL,\nNote: The inputs to the PLC must be provided by the lecturer. Use any PLC source code or PLC software to design a PLC program. The program must be simulated and debugged if any faults. You must simulate the program. Upload the program to a PLC. Connect the output from the PLC to the circuit built for the Electro Technology ISAT.\n3.2 Sub-task 2: Layout and wiring of electrical/electronic circuit\n(Total: 50 marks; 150 minutes)\nActivity 2.1: Enclosure layout and mounting of component, knowledge questionnaire (Sub-total: 15 marks; 30 minutes)\nThe lecturer will give you a circuit diagram and all components needed for the task. You are required to mark off the layout for the push button, toggle switch and female plugs. You must then drill the holes on the enclosure and mount the components. Make sure that all components are mounted securely and that all work done is neatly.\nFinally, you will be issued with a knowledge questionnaire which you must complete and hand back to the lecturer.\nActivity 2.2: Draw a layout diagram of the electronic components on the vero board. Assembling and soldering of the electronic components on the vero board. (Sub-total: 25 marks; 90 minutes)\nYour lecturer will hand out Worksheet 4. Study the circuit diagram carefully and note the layout of components and where all connections must be made. Draw the layout of how you will mount the components on the vero board template and show where the track breaks on the board will be. The lecturer will check your completed layout and then supply you with all components needed for the task.\nYou must then:\n• Adhere to workshop health and safety rules and regulations.\n• Prepare the workstation for a soldering task.\n• Select the correct hand tools required to construct the circuit.\n• Select the required components to construct the circuit.\nRefer to the layout diagram and place the components on the vero board. Mount and solder the components on the vero board.\n• Connect the fly leads from the vero board to the push buttons, toggle switch and female plugs mounted on the enclosure.\nCheck the circuit board for incorrect polarities, solder shorts, dry joints and broken tracks\nActivity 2.3: Knowledge questionnaire (Sub-total: 10 marks; 30 minutes)\nAnswer all the questions on Worksheet 5 and hand in the answer sheet to your lecturer.\n3.3 Sub-task 3: Manufacture a work piece (G-clamp) (Total: 50 marks; 240 minutes) You must manufacture a G-clamp from a given scaled engineering drawing. Activity 3.1: Planning for the manufacturing task (Sub-total: 15 marks; 30 minutes) Study the not-to-scale drawing of the G-clamp.\nCopyright reserved\n4\nPlease turn over\nAnswer the planning-related questions on Worksheet 6.\nNCM MECHATRONICS LEVEL 2 2020\nActivity 3.2: Manufacturing the work piece (Sub-total: 20 marks: 180 minutes)\n-\nAdhere to workshop health and safety rules and regulations.\nStudy the not-to-scale engineering drawing.\nSelect the correct hand tools required to manufacture the work piece. Manufacture the work piece.\nMotte: this activity must be done under supervision and does not need to be completed in one continuous session.\nActfurty 3.3 Knowledge questionnaire (Sub-total: 15 marks: 30 minutes)\nComalete Worksheet 7 under supervision and hand in for marking.\n4\nTime and mark allocation\nActivity\nSit sk\nManufacturing of a work piece\n3\n\t\nme allocation\n\t\nM allocation\n\t\nRisessment tools\n\nCesigning and simulating of a PLC\n\t\n130 minutes\n\t\n50\n\t\nChecklist\n\nprogram and assembling of a personal\n\t\t\t\n\ncomicuter\n\t\n150 minutes\n\t\n50\n\t\nChecklist\n\nLayout of electrical and electronic components and wining of circuit\n2\n\t\n240 minutes\n\t\n50\n\t\nChecklist\n\n(G-damp)\nTOTAL\n\t\n570 minutes\n\t\n150\n\t\n5\nAssessment tools\nUse the checklist on pages 6 as guidance on how your work will be marked.\nPlease turn\nCopyright reserved\n6\n7\n€\n8\nC 9\nNCT MECHATRONICCELSIE 20\nSTUDENT'S CHECKLIST: INTEGRATED SUMMATIVE ASSESSMENT\nCASE\nSub-task 1 Design a PLC program and and assemble computer\n\t\nSUBASKE\n\nUdentify and connect peripherals to computer chassis and bostup Safety-the power was off and unplugged while installing The peripherals were identified on the questionnaire. The peripherals were installed to the correct .\nperipherals\n\t\n\nsicts The computer was correctly tocated un materiall list in Word format and complete the FLC MS Word document was correctly operert Created a table with the correct format and column headings.\nProduce wiring diagram\n\t\n\t\n\t\n\nCorrectly populated of the materialis list.\n\t\n\nDrew a complete electrical circuit on the provided template\n\t\n\nDesign and simulate a PLC program\n\t\n\nA PLC program was designed according to the design brief.\n\t\n\nCorrect inputs and outputs were used according to brief.\n\t\n\nSimulated and debugged the program.\n\t\n\nSUE TOTAL Sub-task 2: Layout and wining of electrical electronic arcut The layout of enclosure and mounting of components\n\t\n\nMeasuring and marking off diane comedly.\n\t\n\nEnclosure was secured for drilling.\n\t\n\nEndosure was drilled to specification. (Holes for push buttons, toodle switch female connectors and the LED.)\n\t\n\nComponents were assembled, fitted to specification and mounted securely.\n\t\n\nThe overall manufacturing process was completed according to sequence plan.\n\t\n\nThe tocis were deaned and returned to the store.\n\t\n\nWork safety and housekeeping were applied throughout the process.\n\t\n\nKnowledge questionnaire\n\t\n\nFive tools were listed.\n\t\n\nVero board layout drawing\n\t\n\nComponents were clearly labelled.\n\t\n\nAll soldering points were indicated on layout\n\t\n\nAll track breaks on vero board were clearly indicated\n\t\n\nClear indication given of where field devices (push buttons, battery, toggle switch)\n\t\n\nmust be connected to vera board.\n\t\n\nThe layout, correct connection and soldering of components\n\t\n[10]\n\nAll solder joints good. Al connections to components tight and secure.\n\t\n\nCorrect operation of circuit.\n\t\n\nSafety and housekeeping\n\t\n\nCorrect PPE worm.\n\t\n\nSafety use of tools (soldering iron).\n\t\nWork place cleaned and tools stored.\nCopyright reserved_\nPlease tum over.....\nNC (V) MECHATRONICS LEVEL 2 2020\nKnowledge questionnaire\n\nAll questions on Worksheet 5 were correctly answered. [50] SUB-TOTAL Sub-task 3. Manufacture a work piece (G-clamp) [15] e questionnaire) to manufacture (Knowledge Listed five processes in the work sequence that will be used to complete the work task.\nPlanning\n\nTool requirements.\n\nSafety requirements.\n\n[20]\nManufacturing work piece\n\nThe materials were correctly cut to size.\n\nThe work piece was de burred.\n\nThe work piece was filed to reference.\n\nThe work piece was measured and scribed.\n\nThe work piece was punched. The drill press was prepared for work.\n\nThe work piece was secured for drilling\n.\n\nThe work piece was drilled to specification\n.\n\nThe ends of the screw thread were drilled to specification.\n\nSafety goggles were worn during drilling operations.\n\nThe work piece was cut to remove excess waste.\n\nwork piece was filed square where the excess waste was removed.\nThe\n\nThe hole for the screw thread was tapped to correct size.\n\nThe work piece was chamfered correctly to specification.\n\nThe screw thread handle was force fitted at one end and a washer was force fitted at the other end.\n\nThe work piece was assembled to specification. The overall manufacturing process was completed according to the sequence plan.\n\n1\nThe tools were cleaned and returned to the store.\n\nWork safety and housekeeping were applied throughout the process.\n\n[15]\nKnowledge questionnaire\n5\n\nQuestion 1.\n\nQuestion 2.\n\n5\nQuestion 3.\n\nSUB-TOTAL50 150 TOTAL\nCopyright reserved\nPlease turn over\nNO MEDHATRONICS\n6. RECORD OF PERFORMANCE IN INTEGRATED SUMMATIVE ASSESSMENELASK\nCollege\nCampus\nStudent's Surmame and First Name/s\nStudent's ID Number\nLecturer's Surname and Initials\nDate of conclusion of assessment\nASSESSMENT GRID\nSUB-TASKS Sub-task 1: Design a PLC program and assemble a computer\n\t\nMARK ALLOCATION\n50\n\t\nSTUDENT'S\nMARK\n\t\nMODERATED\n\nSub-task 2: Layout and wiring of electrical/electronic circuit\n\t\n50\n\t\t\n\nSub-task 3: Manufacture a work piece (G-clamp)\n\t\n50\n\t\t\n\nTotal\n\t\n150\n\t\t\n\nConverted to %\n\t\n100%\n\t\t\n\t\nCOMPETENCE LEVEL INDICATORS\n\t\n\nOutstanding\n\t\nHighly Competent Not yet competent competent\n\t\nNot achieved\n\n80-100%\n\t\n70-79%\n50-69%\n40-49%\n\t\n0-39%\n\n5\n\t\n4\n\t\n3\nStudent's\nCompetence Level\n\t\n1\n\nStudent's Signature\n\t\n\nLecturer's Signature\n\t\n\nDate\n\t\n\nModerator's Signature\n\t\n\nFeedback to student\n\t\nCopyright reserved\n2\n\n"
