ic systems.\n(4) [20]\nQUESTION 5\n5.1\nWhat is the purpose of troubleshooting an electro-pneumatic application? $(1\\times2)$\n(2)\n5.2\nWhat is meant by the commissioning process of an electro-pneumatic application?\n$(1\\times2)$\n(2)\n5.3\nExplain, using a drawing, how you would troubleshoot the pneumatic subsystem of an electro-pneumatic application using the output-input-through- the-system approach.\n$(4+4)$\n(8)\n5.4\nExplain, using a drawing, how you would locate a fault in the electrical control system of an electro-pneumatic application using the half-cut method. ( $(4+4)$\n(8) [20]\n(6040102)\nQUESTION 5\n5.1\n-8-\nNC2100(E)(N11)V\nElectromechanical relays are important elements in mechatronic systems. As a mechatronics technician, you will be expected to troubleshoot on\nelectromechanical relays.\nFIGURE 7 below represents an electromechanical relay.\nSpring\nSwitching Element\nArmature\nNC Contact\nNO Contact\nCommon\nK\nCoil Terminal\nلم\nCoil Terminal\nCoil\nCore FIGURE 7\nNC\nNO\nCOM\nList FIVE possible faults that may be encountered in the relay shown in FIGURE 7.\n(5)\n5.2\nA fault-tree analysis yields a clear logical understanding of troubleshooting a mechatronic element, subsystem or system.\nDetermine the sequence that you would follow to draw up a fault-tree analysis\n(5)\n5.3\nExplain how you would protect a circuit from an overload current.\n(4)\n5.4\nList -FIVE common causes of malfunctions that may occur in a pneumatic control system...\n(5)\n5.5\nWhen the MCB keeps on tripping, what does that tell you about the possible fault that could exist?\n(2)\n[21]\nTOTAL:\n100\nCopyright reserved\nDownloaded from NCV Study Guides App\n1\nAnalyse and connect basic pneumatic technical systems\nBy the end of this topic you will have completed the following:\nSubject Outcomes:\n• describe and apply pneumatic technology\nidentify and apply pneumatic component symbols\nconnect basic pneumatic manual control systems.\nIntroduction\nAs you have already learnt, pneumatic systems use compressed air in systems. Pneumatic power is used in industry, but is also used in applications such as dentistry, construction and mining.\nIn this topic we will describe and apply pneumatic technology. We will also identify and apply pneumatic component symbols and connect basic pneumatic systems.\nTOPIC 3\nPathways to Mechatronic Systems 109\nGlossary\naccumulator, a device used for storing air under pressure used to smooth out surges in the air supply\nactuator: a device that converts pneumatic power into mechanical force and/or motion\nbleeder for the removal of air\ncircuit: an arrangement of interconnected components of parts\ncompressed air. air at any pressure greater than the atmospheric pressure compressor: a device that converts mechanical force and motion into pneumatic power\ncontroller: a device that is used to regulate the function of a component or system\ncylinder a device that converts pneumatic energy into a mechanical linear force or rotary motion\ncylinder (double action): a cylinder that can be operated in both directions cylinder (single action): a cylinder that is actuated in one direction by a pneumatic force and the other by means of a mechanical spring\nelectric control: control actuated electrically\nmanual control: control actuated by the operator\nmechanical control: control actuated by linkages, gears, screws, cams or other mechanical elements\nnomenclature: technical names used in any particular branch of science pneumatic control: control actuated by air or other gas\nDescribe and apply pneumatic technology\nBy the end of this unit you will have completed the following Learning Outcomes and Assessment Standards:\nLearning Outcomes:\nDefine what is meant by the term pneumatics.\nDefine and describe the terms preparation, compressibility, force requirements. noise levels and costs.\nState the four functions of compressed air applications (status of processes, information processing, switching final control 